{s:RC,k:'recap',term:'Recap',say:'Recap in three. Coil current and capacitor voltage carry across a switch. A decay keeps thirty-six point eight percent at tau. And a step runs from start to final.',cues:{r1:'Coil current and',r2:'A decay keeps',r3:'And a step'},panels:[["At the switch","BLU",'ctRecapSwitch',['iL and vC carry over;','L and C become sources at 0⁺.'],'r1'],["Time constant","ORG",'ctRecapDecay',['τ = L/R or RC;','36.8 % is left at t = τ.'],'r2'],["Step response","GRN",'ctRecapStep',['Forced + natural:','from x(0⁺) to x(∞).'],'r3']],live:[0,1,2]},
/* ---- exam practice: the book's own questions and model answers ---- */
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why can the current through an inductor not jump? Answer in terms of energy.",say:'Question one. Why can the current through an inductor not jump? Answer in terms of energy.',cues:{k:'Question one',q:'Why can the'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why can the current through an inductor not jump? Answer in terms of energy.",say:'The inductor stores half L i squared. A jump in current would change that energy in zero time: infinite power. No finite source supplies that, so the current is continuous.',cues:{a1:'The inductor stores',a2:'A jump in',a3:'No finite source'},al:[["The stored energy is ½LiL².",'a1'],["A jump in iL would change it in zero time: infinite power.",'a2'],["No finite source supplies that, so iL is continuous.",'a3']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),CT_T(310,44,'stored energy w = ½Li²',{fs:26,fw:800,a:'middle'})+CT_L(60,420,600,420,CT_AX,2)+CT_L(60,80,60,420,CT_AX,2)+CT_L(250,80,250,430,CT_AX,2,'stroke-dasharray="6 6"')+CT_T(250,456,'t = 0',{fs:22,a:'middle',fill:CT_MUT})+CT_T(70,104,'w',{fs:24,fw:700,fill:CT_MUT,it:1}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),CT_poly([[60,340],[250,340],[250,150],[590,150]],TRED,5)+CT_T(270,132,'jump: p = dw/dt = ∞',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:TRED})+CT_L(232,232,268,268,TRED,5)+CT_L(268,232,232,268,TRED,5))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a3'),CT_curve(t=>t<0?0:1-Math.exp(-t/0.9),-1.5,3.5,60,590,340,170,GRN,6)+CT_T(330,300,'finite p: w rises smoothly',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:GRN})+CT_T(330,500,'so iL cannot jump',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:GRN}))}`},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A 10 V DC source feeds R = 5 Ω and L = 0.5 H in series through a switch that closes at t = 0, with iL(0⁻) = 0. Find vL(0⁺) and di/dt at 0⁺.",say:'Question two. Ten volts, five ohms and half a henry in series, switched on with no current. Find the inductor voltage and the slope at zero plus.',cues:{k:'Question two',q:'Ten volts'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A 10 V DC source feeds R = 5 Ω and L = 0.5 H in series through a switch that closes at t = 0, with iL(0⁻) = 0. Find vL(0⁺) and di/dt at 0⁺.",say:`At zero plus the current is still zero, so the resistor drops nothing and ${P('KVL')} puts all ten volts on the inductor. The slope: ten over zero point five, twenty amps per second.`,cues:{fk:'At zero plus',k1:'At zero plus',k1b:'so the resistor',k2:'puts all ten',k2b:'on the inductor',k3:'The slope',k3b:'twenty amps per'},calc:{f:['iL(0⁺) = iL(0⁻)','di/dt = vL(0⁺)/L'],hint:'At 0⁺ the inductor is a 0 A source.',fk:'fk',steps:[["Current at 0⁺",[`${CT_sub('i','L')}(0⁺) = ${CT_sub('i','L')}(0⁻) = 0`,`${CT_sub('v','R')}(0⁺) = 5 × 0 = 0 V`],["k1","k1b"]],["KVL",[`10 = 0 + ${CT_sub('v','L')}(0⁺)`,`${CT_sub('v','L')}(0⁺) = 10 V`],["k2","k2b"]],["First slope",["di/dt = 10 / 0.5","di/dt = 20 A/s"],["k3","k3b"]]],live:[0,1,2]}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"A 12 V DC source is switched at t = 0 onto R = 6 Ω, L = 2 H and C = 0.5 F in series, with nothing stored. At 0⁺ the full 12 V appears across L although vC = 0 and i = 0. Explain, and find di/dt at 0⁺, i(∞) and vC(∞).",say:'Question three. A series R L C circuit with nothing stored meets twelve volts. Why does the inductor take all twelve at zero plus? Find the slope and final values.',cues:{k:'Question three',q:'A series R'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"A 12 V DC source is switched at t = 0 onto R = 6 Ω, L = 2 H and C = 0.5 F in series, with nothing stored. At 0⁺ the full 12 V appears across L although vC = 0 and i = 0. Explain, and find di/dt at 0⁺, i(∞) and vC(∞).",say:`Nothing stored means zero capacitor voltage and zero current, so ${P('KVL')} puts all twelve volts on the inductor. The slope is twelve over two: six amps per second, steep but continuous. Finally the capacitor is open: zero current, twelve volts.`,cues:{a1:'Nothing stored means',a2:'The slope is',a3:'Finally the capacitor'},al:[["vC(0⁺) = 0 and i(0⁺) = 0: no drop on C or R, so KVL puts 12 V on L.",'a1'],["di/dt = 12/2 = 6 A/s: steep but continuous.",'a2'],["At ∞, C is open: i(∞) = 0 A and vC(∞) = 12 V.",'a3']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),CT_T(310,34,'at 0⁺, nothing stored',{fs:26,fw:800,a:'middle'})+CT_vs(80,230,32)+CT_L(80,198,80,100,CT_INK,3)+CT_L(80,262,80,360,CT_INK,3)+CT_T(126,240,'12 V',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:ORG})+CT_L(80,100,160,100,CT_INK,3)+CT_res(160,420,100)+CT_L(420,100,520,100,CT_INK,3)+CT_coilV(520,100,360,GRN)+CT_L(80,360,220,360,CT_INK,3)+CT_cap(220,380,360)+CT_L(380,360,520,360,CT_INK,3)+CT_T(290,74,'6 Ω: i = 0, drop 0',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'middle',fill:CT_MUT})+CT_T(300,408,'0.5 F: vC = 0',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'middle',fill:CT_MUT})+CT_T(496,216,'2 H',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'end',fill:CT_MUT})+CT_T(496,256,'vL = 12 V',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'end',fill:GRN}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),CT_arrow(250,160,350,160,ORG,4)+CT_T(300,196,'di/dt = 6 A/s',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:ORG}))}
${g('rise',tk(c,'a3'),CT_T(310,452,'at ∞: C open, i = 0, vC = 12 V',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:BLU}))}`},
{s:LS,k:'figs',term:'Your notes',title:'For your notes',say:'Your free notes are the PDF in the description. Redraw the three switching circuits and the decay with its tangent.',cues:{t:'Your free notes',f:'Redraw the three'},tags:[["Free notes for this lesson (PDF)", "link in the description"], ["The three chapters", "Initial and final conditions; source-free RL and RC circuits and the time constant; first-order step response"], ["Key terms and glossary", "at the end of the notes"]],figs:[["Before, just after, long after", "Before: 2 A and 6 V. At 0⁺: L a 2 A source, C a 6 V source, 4 V on L. At ∞: L short, C open."], ["The decay and its tangent", "i = 2e⁻²⁰⁰ᵗ A: the tangent at t = 0 hits zero at τ = 5 ms; 0.2 A is reached at 11.5 ms."], ["Forced plus natural", "10 V forced plus −10e⁻¹⁰ᵗ V natural gives vC = 10(1 − e⁻¹⁰ᵗ) V, 6.32 V at τ = 0.1 s."]],note:"Sources your free notes draw on: William H. Hayt (Engineering Circuit Analysis: chapters on transient analysis of first-order and second-order circuits and on the Laplace transform method)"},
{s:OUT,k:'outro',term:'Outro',say:'So remember: coil current and capacitor voltage carry over, a decay keeps thirty-six point eight percent at tau, and a step runs from start to final. Next time, second-order RLC circuits. See you there!',cues:{f:'So remember',w1:'coil current and',w2:'a decay keeps',w3:'and a step runs',n:'Next time',s:'See you there'},words:[['iL AND vC CARRY OVER','BLU','w1'],['36.8 % LEFT AT τ','ORG','w2'],['x(0⁺) → x(∞)','GRN','w3']],next:["NEXT TIME", "Next lesson", "Second-order RLC circuits: overdamped, critically damped, underdamped", "book"],end:'See you there!'}
